  "steps": [
    {
      "text": "Cook 150 g pancetta, finely chopped with 2 tbsp olive oil in a heavy pot over medium for 5 minutes, until the fat renders. Add 1 onion, finely chopped, 1 carrot, finely chopped and 1 celery stalk, finely chopped and cook 10 minutes, until soft and golden.",
      "ingredientRefs": [
        0,
        1,
        2,
        3,
        4
      ],
      "timers": [
        {
          "text": "5 minutes",
          "seconds": 300
        },
        {
          "text": "10 minutes",
          "seconds": 600
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Raise the heat, add 500 g coarsely ground beef chuck and 250 g ground pork and cook 10 minutes, breaking it up, until the liquid has evaporated and the meat browns.",
      "ingredientRefs": [
        5,
        6
      ],
      "timers": [
        {
          "text": "10 minutes",
          "seconds": 600
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Add 180 ml dry white wine and simmer 5 minutes, until evaporated. Stir in 400 g tomato passata, 1 tbsp tomato paste and half of 500 ml beef or chicken stock.",
      "ingredientRefs": [
        7,
        8,
        9,
        10
      ],
      "timers": [
        {
          "text": "5 minutes",
          "seconds": 300
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Simmer uncovered on the lowest heat for 2 hours 30 minutes, adding stock as it thickens. Stir in 180 ml whole milk for the last 30 minutes. Season with 1 1/2 tsp salt and 1/2 tsp black pepper. It should be thick and rich, not soupy.",
      "ingredientRefs": [
        11,
        12,
        13
      ],
      "timers": [
        {
          "text": "2 hours 30 minutes",
          "seconds": 9000
        },
        {
          "text": "30 minutes",
          "seconds": 1800
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Make the béchamel: melt 80 g butter over medium, whisk in 80 g all-purpose flour and cook 2 minutes. Whisk in 1 L whole milk, warmed gradually and simmer 8 minutes, whisking, until it coats a spoon. Season with 1/4 tsp grated nutmeg and 1 tsp salt (for the béchamel).",
      "ingredientRefs": [
        14,
        15,
        16,
        17,
        18
      ],
      "timers": [
        {
          "text": "2 minutes",
          "seconds": 120
        },
        {
          "text": "8 minutes",
          "seconds": 480
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Heat the oven to 180°C. If the sheets of 500 g fresh egg lasagna sheets (or spinach sheets for lasagne verdi) are fresh, blanch them 30 seconds in boiling salted water and lay them on towels.",
      "ingredientRefs": [
        19
      ],
      "timers": [
        {
          "text": "30 seconds",
          "seconds": 30
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "In a 23 × 33 cm dish, spread a thin layer of béchamel, then layer pasta, ragù, béchamel and a handful of 150 g Parmigiano-Reggiano, grated. Repeat for 5 or 6 layers, finishing with béchamel and Parmigiano. Dot with 2 tbsp butter, in small pieces.",
      "ingredientRefs": [
        20,
        21
      ],
      "timers": []
    },
    {
      "text": "Bake 40 minutes, until bubbling and golden-brown on top. Rest 20 minutes before cutting.",
      "ingredientRefs": [],
      "timers": [
        {
          "text": "40 minutes",
          "seconds": 2400
        },
        {
          "text": "20 minutes",
          "seconds": 1200
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "notes": "In Bologna the layers are thin and many: a light hand with each sauce makes a lasagna that holds its shape. The milk in the ragù softens the acidity and tenderizes the meat.\n\nDried lasagna sheets work: use no-boil sheets or boil regular ones 2 minutes less than the package says. The whole dish can be assembled a day ahead and baked from the fridge (add 15 minutes). The ragù alone, with tagliatelle, is the real tagliatelle alla bolognese.",
  "servings": 8,
